<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy — the iOS app.

  App Store submission is rejected without one of these, so it is here for the
  same reason the entitlements file is: the app does not ship without it.

  What it says is short because the game genuinely collects nothing. There is
  no server, no account and no analytics: the colony is a JSON file in an App
  Group container, photographs stay in the player's own library, and the only
  thing that ever leaves the device is a `.flower`, `.swarm` or `.flowerhive`
  file the player hands to somebody through the share sheet. So
  NSPrivacyTracking is false, NSPrivacyTrackingDomains is empty, and there are
  no collected data types to declare — including location, which the game reads
  but never sends anywhere. See `FlowerShare` for the one place a coordinate
  can leave the device, and for why it is off by default.

  NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes is the part that must be kept in step with the
  code. It lists required-reason APIs the app actually calls, and Apple checks
  it against the binary. Grepped rather than guessed:

  - UserDefaults, category CA92.1. Reached through `@AppStorage` in
    `SettingsView`, `ContentView`, `ColonyDashboardView`, `CollectionView`,
    `ShareFlowerView`, `RequestViews` and `SwarmShareViews`, and directly in
    `BackgroundRefresh`. All of it `UserDefaults.standard`, all of it the
    app's own settings — the hum, the digest hour, the hemisphere, the name
    the player shares under. CA92.1 is exactly that: defaults only this app
    can read. Note that the App Group is used for the save *file* and not for
    defaults, which would be a different reason code.

  - File timestamps, category C617.1. `GamePersistence.changeToken()` reads
    the save file's modification date through `attributesOfItem`, so that a
    `GameStore` can tell when a notification button, a widget button or Siri
    wrote the file behind its back and reload before it advances. C617.1 is
    the reason for a file inside the app's own container, which is what the
    save is. (`PhotoLibrary` reads `PHAsset.creationDate`, which is a photo
    library property rather than a file attribute and needs no declaration.)

  Deliberately absent, having been looked for:

  - System boot time (35F9.1). No `systemUptime`, no `ProcessInfo` clock.
  - Disk space (E174.1). Nothing asks how much room is left.
  - Active keyboards, and the whole of the tracking-domain machinery.

  If any of those four ever gets used, this file has to change with it, and the
  failure mode is a rejected build rather than a compiler error. That is the
  note in SETUP.md.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
